{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he said, Now also according to your words, so it is.</b> How could he say 'according to your words, so it is'? For they said that the thief is deserving of death and they would be slaves, yet he did not say so — rather, 'the one with whom it is found shall be my slave, and you shall be clear' (Genesis 44:10). And Rashi's interpretation does not accord with the precise language. Moreover, how could the emissary say 'he shall be my slave'? He should have said 'he shall be a slave to my lord,' for he himself is a slave, and if so he made them a slave to slaves. Furthermore, he said 'and you shall be clear,' whereas Joseph said 'and you, go up in peace to your father' (Genesis 44:17). And what is the force of 'you shall be clear'? He should have said 'and you are clear.' Also the expression 'both we, and also the one in whose hand the goblet was found' (Genesis 44:16) requires explanation. And in the Akeidah they explained that they said: We know that this offense is not in us, except that Elohim has found the offense of your servants, and another sin caused us this stumbling — and that is the sin of selling Joseph, in which Benjamin had no share. Therefore they said: Not only — it is obvious to us that we deserve to be slaves for the sin of selling our brother into slavery; but even also this — the one in whose hand the goblet was found, who has no share in that sin — he too shall be a slave. But according to that explanation it is difficult: if so, the ruler would not know or understand their words, and what 'not only this, even that' is there, for without doubt they did not reveal to him the selling of their brother." "Therefore I must explain the 'not only this, even that' in a sense that the ruler too understood, for there were four divisions and rulings here. For at first the brothers exceeded the measure, saying that the thief himself is deserving of death, and his accomplices deserve a lighter punishment — therefore they shall be slaves. For they should have stood watch, since this was evidently a habitual thief, given that he could not restrain himself from stealing even from the ruler's table. And Joseph's emissary said that in our law the thief is not deserving of death, and we do not impose the death penalty for theft of money but only servitude. And according to this, it is just that you all be slaves; but since it would be a disgrace to you to equate you in one judgment with the thief himself, lest people say that you too are thieves like him — therefore I will make this second distinction. And even according to this distinction it will be 'according to your words,' that you will not be equal to the thief himself: for he shall be my slave, that is, a slave to slaves, and you too shall be slaves — but not to me, rather to the ruler himself, that is, servitude in a clean manner. Therefore he said 'you shall be clear' — there would indeed be a state of servitude for you, but in a somewhat clean manner. And he hinted to them that they would be 'clear' of their property, for whatever a slave acquires, his master acquires — as Rashi explained on the verse (Exodus 21:28) 'and the owner of the ox is clear' — so-and-so has gone out clear of his possessions. Thus he hinted to them that they would indeed have servitude, but it would not entail the kinds of degradation attached to servitude — only this alone, that they would be cleared of their property and would not be free men. But the thief himself, besides this, has many additional kinds of disgrace attached to his being a slave to slaves. And because he did not equate, even according to his own reasoning, the brothers with the thief himself, he said 'now also' — according to my reasoning, 'according to your words, so it is.' And the brothers, when they came before Joseph, said 'Behold, we are slaves to my lord,' as your servant decreed upon us. But what he decreed — that the thief would be a slave to slaves — that is a great disgrace. And concerning this they asked the ruler to lighten their burden, and by way of compromise they were stringent upon themselves to equate themselves with him, that they would all be slaves to the ruler, for there is no need to suspect that he would turn and steal, since we will stand watch. And they spoke in the manner of 'not only this, even that': 'also we' — not only we; it is obvious that we should not be a slave to slaves, for we are not suspected in your eyes, since 'the silver we found in the mouth of our sacks we brought back to you, and how could we steal from your lord's house?' (Genesis 44:8). But even 'also the one in whose hand the goblet was found' (Genesis 44:16), we seek to do him a kindness, that he be a slave to my lord and not a slave to slaves. And Joseph said: 'Far be it from me to equate you with the thief; rather, he shall be my own slave, as you requested — and if so, perforce you must go up in peace to your father.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:34.374485808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}